course: biology
lesson: 270
title: Not dinosaurs at all: pterosaurs, plesiosaurs, mosasaurs and Mary Anning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Toy boxes and cartoons call every big old reptile a dinosaur, but scientists have a rule: a dinosaur stood with its legs straight under its body, not sprawled out to the side like a lizard's.
explain: So the flying pterosaurs and the swimming plesiosaurs, mosasaurs and ichthyosaurs were not dinosaurs, even though they lived at the same time.
explain: A pterosaur's wing was skin stretched from one hugely long fourth finger, and the biggest, Quetzalcoatlus, stood as tall as a giraffe and strode about on all fours like a giant stork.
explain: Long before scientists understood any of this, a girl called Mary Anning in Lyme Regis, England, dug out the first complete ichthyosaur at about 12, and spent her life finding fossils on the stormy beaches.
explain: In The Flintstones cavemen keep dinosaurs as pets, but no person ever saw a living non-bird dinosaur: they were gone 66 million years before the first humans.
fact: Pterosaurs, often called pterodactyls, were flying reptiles, close cousins of dinosaurs but not dinosaurs, and the first animals with backbones to fly.
fact: A pterosaur wing was skin stretched from a very long fourth finger.
fact: Quetzalcoatlus stood as tall as a giraffe, with wings about 10 metres across; it walked on all fours and caught small animals on land, and probably vaulted into the air with its strong arms.
fact: Plesiosaurs were sea reptiles with four flippers that died out 66 million years ago; a fossil mother with a baby inside shows they gave birth in the sea.
fact: The famous 1934 Loch Ness monster photo was a fake made with a toy, and a big reptile could not live in a cold lake with so little food.
fact: Mosasaurs were huge sea lizards, up to about 15 metres long, related to monitor lizards and snakes, with a second row of teeth in the roof of the mouth; the first was found in the Netherlands in the 1770s.
fact: Ichthyosaurs, the «fish lizards», looked like dolphins but were reptiles, and one kind had eyes about as big as a football, among the biggest eyes of any animal, for seeing in deep, dark water.
fact: Mary Anning of Lyme Regis found the first complete ichthyosaur when she was about 12; she became an expert fossil hunter but got little credit in her time, because the scientists of her day did not take seriously a working woman who had never been to university, and today she has a statue.
fact: Dimetrodon, with its sail, lived about 280 million years ago, before any dinosaurs, and is closer to mammals, and so to us, than to dinosaurs.
fact: Dinosaurs had legs straight under the body, which shows as a hole in the hip socket; flying and swimming reptiles do not count as dinosaurs.
fact: Woolly mammoths were mammals with fur that fed their babies milk; they lived until about 4,000 years ago and never met dinosaurs.
fact: Humans appeared about 300,000 years ago, while the non-bird dinosaurs died out 66 million years ago.
exercise: Were pterosaurs dinosaurs? | No, they were flying reptiles, cousins of dinosaurs | Yes, they were the biggest of all the flying dinosaurs | No, they were giant bats, which are furry mammals | No, they were early birds with feathers and teeth
exercise: What held up a pterosaur's wing? | A very long fourth finger | Long feathers along its arm bones | Its long, stiff bony tail held out | A spare bone from its back
exercise: Which were the first animals with backbones to fly? | Pterosaurs | Bats | Birds | Flying fish
exercise: How tall was Quetzalcoatlus standing on the ground? | As tall as a giraffe | As tall as a garden pigeon | As tall as a house cat | As tall as a ten-storey block
exercise: How did Quetzalcoatlus get into the air? | It probably vaulted up with its strong arms | It could only jump off the tops of high cliffs | It ran fast on two legs, like a plane on a runway | It could never take off and only walked about
exercise: How did plesiosaurs have their babies? | They gave birth in the sea | They laid eggs on sandy beaches | They laid eggs high in trees | They laid eggs in shallow rivers
exercise: How many flippers did a plesiosaur have? | Four | Two | Six | None
exercise: What was the famous 1934 Loch Ness photo? | A fake made with a toy | A real plesiosaur swimming | A real whale that got lost | A floating log by chance
exercise: Which living animals are mosasaurs related to? | Monitor lizards and snakes | Sharks and rays | Dolphins and whales | Crocodiles and alligators
exercise: What did mosasaurs have in the roof of the mouth? | A second row of teeth | A blowhole for breathing | A tongue like a frog's | Nothing at all, just bone
exercise: What did ichthyosaurs look like? | Dolphins, though they were reptiles | Turtles with very long necks | Giant crabs with hard shells | Flying lizards with skin wings
exercise: Why did ichthyosaurs have such big eyes? | To see in deep, dark water | To scare off bigger enemies | To see the stars at night better | To store food for later in winter
exercise: What did Mary Anning find when she was about 12? | The first complete ichthyosaur | The first T. rex | A living plesiosaur in a rock pool | A box of gold coins
exercise: Was Dimetrodon a dinosaur? | No, it lived before dinosaurs and is closer to mammals | Yes, it was the oldest dinosaur, the first of them all | Yes, a flying one that used its sail as a wing | No, it was a fish with a sail that lived in the sea
exercise: What is the rule for being a dinosaur? | Legs held straight under the body | Being very big and very heavy | Having scales all over the body | Living a very long time ago
exercise: What were woolly mammoths? | Mammals | Dinosaurs | Reptiles | Birds
exercise: Did cavemen fight dinosaurs? | No, dinosaurs died out 66 million years before people | Yes, all the time, with long spears and heavy clubs and stones | Yes, but only the small ones that came near caves | Only in Europe, where the last few dinosaurs lived
test: «a pterodactyl is a flying dinosaur, everyone knows that» What is right? | No, pterosaurs were flying reptiles, not dinosaurs | Yes, it was the very biggest flying dinosaur of all | Yes, it was a kind of bird with feathers and teeth | No, it was really a big bat, a furry mammal
test: In The Flintstones, Fred rides a dinosaur to work. What is true? | No person ever saw a living non-bird dinosaur | Cavemen did tame dinosaurs and used them for work | Dinosaurs lived until only about 1,000 years ago | Some people in far-off places still ride them
test: «there's a plesiosaur in Loch Ness, my uncle saw the photo» What is better? | The famous photo was a fake, and the cold lake has too little food | Yes, the photo proves it, because a camera cannot lie about a thing | Yes, plesiosaurs still live in all the deepest lakes of the world | No, it was a crocodile that swam all the way up from Africa
test: Count them: a toy set has 2 Quetzalcoatlus and 3 plesiosaurs. How many wings and flippers are there in all? | 4 wings and 12 flippers | 2 wings and 6 flippers | 4 wings and 6 flippers | 6 wings and 12 flippers
test: In a film a giant mosasaur leaps out of a pool and swallows a shark. What is true about mosasaurs? | They were huge sea lizards up to about 15 metres, related to snakes | They were dinosaurs that swam, the biggest dinosaurs that ever lived | They were sharks themselves, with skeletons made of soft cartilage | They were small, about the size of a cat, and ate only little shrimps
test: What would happen if a plesiosaur had to crawl up a beach to lay eggs, like a turtle? | It would be clumsy on land, but it did not need to: it gave birth in the sea | It would fly back to the sea | It would turn into a dinosaur | It would lay eggs as big as a house
test: «Dimetrodon's my fave dinosaur, the one with the sail» What is true? | It is not a dinosaur; it is closer to us than to dinosaurs | It is the biggest dinosaur of all, bigger than any sauropod | It is a flying dinosaur that used its sail as a wing | It is a fish with a sail that lived in the deep sea
test: Why was Mary Anning given little credit in her own time? | Scientists of her time gave little credit to a working woman without a university | She found nothing important, only a few small shells that anyone could pick up | She hid all her fossils away and never let anyone see them | She was too young to dig, so her brother did all of the work
test: «mammoths and T. rex, who would win» What is wrong with the question? | They never met; mammoths lived 66 million years later | Nothing, they lived side by side and often fought over food | Mammoths were dinosaurs too, so it was a fair fight | T. rex was a mammal, so they were close cousins
test: A scientist checks the hip socket of a new fossil and finds a hole in it. What does it suggest? | The legs stood straight under the body, like a dinosaur's | The animal could fly, because a hole makes the hip lighter | The animal lived in water and swam with its back legs | The animal had no legs and slid along like a snake
test: Why could a giant Quetzalcoatlus catch prey on land? | It strode on all fours like a huge stork | It dug long tunnels to trap its prey | It swam underground through soft mud | It hid in caves and leapt out at prey
exam: Identify the classification of the pterosaurs as presently accepted. | Flying reptiles closely related to, but distinct from, the dinosaurs | Dinosaurs adapted to flight, and the direct ancestors of all birds | Early mammals resembling bats, with wings of skin and fur | Primitive birds possessing feathers, teeth and clawed wings
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reproduction of plesiosaurs. | Fossil evidence demonstrates that they gave birth to live young at sea | They are understood to have laid eggs upon beaches, in the manner of turtles | They are understood to have laid eggs in nests constructed in trees | Nothing whatever is known of their reproduction, as no young are preserved
exam: Ascertain the anatomical criterion by which dinosaurs are distinguished from other ancient reptiles. | Limbs held erect beneath the body, attested by an open hip socket | Great size exceeding that of any other reptile, living or extinct | The possession of scales covering the whole of the body | A habitat confined to water, with limbs modified into flippers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of Mary Anning? | She discovered the first complete ichthyosaur in childhood and became an accomplished collector of fossils | She was the first to describe Tyrannosaurus rex, from bones she found herself upon the coast of Dorset in England | She disproved the existence of extinct animals by showing that all fossils belonged to living kinds | She was the first person to fly an aircraft over the cliffs of Dorset
